Main purpose is to reduce the uncertainty of how to initialise judgements. DHO follows a similar structure already - the `apply()` method was intended to be made public when pooling is implemented.

I haven't looked at this PR properly until today, as I was mostly waiting for the framework-side change to go in. There I just mostly looked at it and went "yep, it's a drawable pool" and figured it was sane based off the test cases, which was maybe a little naive. I did look at this today but not too thoroughly and my focus is off so this might not be the most useful opinion ever.

I do agree with the assessment that the PR in its initial state could be improved upon as the drawable preparation logic was a little scattered, as @smoogipoo said. The [first proposal](https://github.com/ppy/osu/pull/9431#issuecomment-656074008) I like for how clean it is in this case, but in general I don't think I necessarily agree with tying pooled drawables to a model class, which might not be all that valid always (thinking of something like particle effects - not much reason to force creation/passing of models for something that isn't really physical representation of data). While we could have pools for model-less drawables and pools for model-backed drawables that just feels like enterprise-grade design™ so probably not a good idea.

The [second patch](https://github.com/ppy/osu/pull/9431#issuecomment-656112874) I would wholeheartedly agree with and while it doesn't iron out all of the kinks it's definitely better. Although maybe I'd expand on that version in the following manner:

